Introduction
Background
Parkinson disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with motor manifestations that include
bradykinesia, tremor, rigidity, gait disturbance, and balance impairment [1]. It contributes substantially to the
global burden of neurological disease, and its rising prevalence has been framed as a “Parkinson pandemic”
requiring scalable public-health responses [2, 3]. Despite advances in digital assessment and biomarker
research, PD diagnosis remains primarily clinical, relying on specialist neurological examination guided by
criteria such as the Movement Disorder Society Clinical Diagnostic Criteria for Parkinson’s Disease [4].
However, clinicopathological evidence indicates that diagnostic accuracy varies according to clinical setting,
examiner expertise, and duration of follow-up [5]. In community and pre-referral settings, subtle or
intermittent motor changes may be overlooked or misattributed to ageing, musculoskeletal problems, essential
tremor, or other common conditions [6, 7]. This creates a measurement gap between the need for scalable
early motor assessment and the limited availability of specialist neurological expertise.
