Smartphone-based sensing offers a scalable approach for collecting objective motor data beyond specialist
clinical settings. Embedded inertial measurement units (IMUs) can quantify acceleration and angular velocity
during tremor, standing balance, and gait tasks, and prior wearable- and smartphone-based studies support the
relevance of these motion signals for PD classification and early motor assessment [8, 9]. Recent reviews of
machine-learning and deep-learning studies in PD further highlight the range of available datasets, algorithms,
sensing modalities, and generalisation challenges in digital motor-assessment research [10-13]. Annotated
smartwatch datasets also illustrate the importance of clinically structured and well-labelled sensor data when
developing machine-learning models for movement disorders [14]. In addition to inertial sensing, smartphone
touchscreen interactions provide a complementary source of fine-motor information. Touchscreen typing,
touch-gesture, smartphone finger-drawing, and handwriting/dexterity studies suggest that movement timing,
scaling accuracy, drawing trajectories, and fine-motor traces can contain PD-relevant motor information,
including signals related to bradykinesia, dexterity impairment, and early motor dysfunction [15-18].
From a clinical perspective, motor impairment in PD is multidimensional and cannot be adequately captured
by a single sign or task [19]. Rest tremor may be intermittent or absent, postural tremor may overlap with
other tremor syndromes, gait impairment may reflect bradykinesia, reduced arm swing, freezing tendency,
balance dysfunction, or non-neurological comorbidity, and fine-motor impairment may be influenced by
bradykinesia, rigidity, dystonia, arthritis, or task familiarity [20]. Smartphone-based motor assessment
therefore requires interpretation across complementary motor domains rather than reliance on any single
digital task. This multidomain structure also creates an analytical challenge: clinically relevant information
may be contained not only in summary measures, but also in the temporal organisation of tremor, balance,
gait, and touchscreen-interaction signals. Deep sequence models are well suited to this setting because they
can analyse these temporal patterns while preserving more of the original motor time-series structure than
handcrafted features alone.
These clinical and signal-level complexities require careful machine-learning design to avoid
overinterpretation. Recurrent neural-network architectures, including LSTM- and GRU-based models, have
previously been explored for PD classification using sequential motor data [21], supporting the relevance of
sequence modelling in this field. However, applying deep sequence models to smartphone-derived PD motor
measures raises several important considerations. First, PD motor manifestations are multidomain and
heterogeneous, so single-task models may miss complementary information from tremor, standing balance,
gait, and fine-dexterity tasks. Second, windowed sensor pipelines are vulnerable to data leakage if segments
from the same participant appear in both training and evaluation partitions. In repeated-measures digital health
datasets, participant-aware validation is essential because windows, trials, or sessions from the same
participant are correlated and can leak participant-specific information across folds if split at the sample level
