Third, deep learning models applied to correlated sensor streams require cautious interpretability analysis
because feature-attribution methods describe model reliance rather than causal physiology. Shapley additive
explanations (SHAP) provide a framework for prediction attribution [24], but dependence among features can
affect Shapley-value approximations and interpretation [25].
Objectives
Therefore, the objective of this study was to evaluate whether recurrent and convolutional-recurrent deep
sequence models can learn Parkinson disease–related motor information directly from brief CheckPD App
smartphone IMU and touchscreen motor-task time series. Specifically, we compared BiLSTM and Conv1D
BiLSTM architectures across individual tremor, balance, gait, and PinchToSize tasks; evaluated multimodal
neural stacking across complementary motor domains; and examined post hoc model-reliance patterns. The
inclusion of PinchToSize allowed the analysis to extend beyond inertial sensing to touchscreen-based fine
motor dynamics, including scaling accuracy, timing, and adjustment behaviour. This study should be
interpreted as a leakage-aware internal validation of deep sequence modelling for smartphone-derived motor
time series, rather than as evidence of calibrated clinical risk prediction, operating-threshold development,
differential-diagnosis validation, or workflow implementation.
Methods
Study Design and Participants
This was a retrospective case-control modelling analysis using a curated, labeled subset of smartphone
derived motor assessment data collected through the CheckPD App, a national digital PD screening
programme in Thailand. The broader CheckPD programme has been implemented in collaboration with the
Thai Red Cross Society and the National Health Security Office, and has been delivered through multiple real
world pathways, including the Parkinson’s Disease Clinic at Chulalongkorn Centre of Excellence for
Parkinson’s Disease and Related Disorders (ChulaPD), field screening campaigns, online training-activated
screening sessions, Village Health Volunteer (VHV)-assisted community screening, and self-initiated app
downloads [26]. Data for the present analysis were collected between January 2024 and April 2026 and
exported from the CheckPD analytics infrastructure after de-identification. Details regarding programme
registration, ethics approval, informed consent, data protection, and data availability are provided in the
Protocol and Registration, Ethical Considerations, and Data Availability sections.
