The present analytic cohort comprised 679 labeled participants with sufficient task data for the selected
analyses, including 344 participants with neurologist-confirmed PD and 335 neurologist-confirmed healthy
controls. PD diagnoses were established by certified neurologists according to the Movement Disorder
Society Clinical Diagnostic Criteria for PD [4]. Healthy controls were selected from the available neurologist
confirmed control pool to approximate the PD group by age and gender before model development, thereby
reducing, but not eliminating, demographic confounding.
PD participants were recruited through the CheckPD programme, including participants from the PD Clinic at
ChulaPD and participants identified or assessed through CheckPD screening workflows. When available,
recruitment source was categorised as clinic-based, field/community screening, online training-activated
screening, VHV-assisted screening, or self-initiated app use. Among the 344 PD participants, 120 were
previously diagnosed clinic-based PD participants and 224 were identified or assessed through CheckPD
field/community workflows. Because recruitment pathway may be associated with disease severity, task
supervision, and task completion, recruitment-source heterogeneity was considered a limitation of this
retrospective analysis. Additionally, separate counts for online training-activated screening, Village Health
Volunteer–assisted screening, and self-initiated app use were not consistently available in the de-identified
analytic dataset and therefore could not be reported. Healthy controls were independent field/community
participants or relatives recruited through the CheckPD screening workflow. Participants were excluded from
the healthy-control pool if they had a diagnosed movement disorder or if neurologist assessment identified PD
or clinically evident parkinsonism. The comparison group should therefore be interpreted as neurologist
confirmed healthy controls rather than as an unverified non-PD group. However, controls were not selected to
represent a structured symptomatic differential-diagnosis cohort, and the dataset did not include separate
confirmed subgroups for essential tremor, atypical parkinsonism, drug-induced parkinsonism, neuropathy,
frailty, arthritis, or musculoskeletal gait disorders.
Eligibility Criteria, Exclusions, and Clinical Characterisation
Eligibility for the source CheckPD programme and inclusion in the curated modelling dataset required all of
the following: age 40 years or older at screening; digital informed consent within the CheckPD application; an
available diagnostic label for the curated modelling dataset; and
evaluable data for at least one of the five analysed motor tasks. For task-specific analyses, records were
excluded if consent was incomplete or withdrawn, if technical failure prevented capture of evaluable data, or
if the corresponding task recording did not pass quality control.
