Protocol and Registration
The broader CheckPD/Parkinson’s Disease Risk Survey programme was registered in the Thai Clinical Trials
Registry (TCTR20231025005) and on ClinicalTrials.gov as an observational patient registry (NCT06609681).
The present study is a retrospective model-development and internal-validation analysis of de-identified
CheckPD App data conducted under the ethics-approved broader CheckPD programme. It was not registered
as a separate clinical trial, and no separate publicly registered prospective analysis protocol was prepared for
this retrospective modelling analysis.
Ethical Considerations
The broader CheckPD programme and the present retrospective de-identified modelling analysis were
conducted under ethics approval from the Institutional Review Board of the Faculty of Medicine,
Chulalongkorn University (IRB No. 0804/65), and the Central Research Ethics Committee (CREC) under the
approved protocol entitled “Flipping the Paradigm of Parkinson’s Disease: A Model of National ‘Eat, Move,
Sleep’ Digital Interventions to Prevent or Slow the Rise of Non-Communicable Diseases in Thailand” (CREC
No. CREC023/68BR-MED06; Certificate No. COA-CREC105/2025). Digital informed consent was obtained
from all participants through the CheckPD App before screening or assessment. The present analysis used de
identified data exported from the CheckPD analytics infrastructure. Data handling was conducted in
accordance with institutional governance requirements and Thailand’s Personal Data Protection Act.
Smartphone Assessment Protocol
Data were collected through the CheckPD mobile application using either participants' personal smartphones
or programme-provided smartphones as part of the broader national screening workflow. However, device
ownership status was not consistently retained in the de-identified analytic dataset; therefore, the proportions
of personal versus programme-provided devices could not be determined. Operating system, device
manufacturer, and specific smartphone model were also not systematically available at the participant or
recording level. The full CheckPD workflow includes app access, user registration, digital informed consent,
guided digital motor and voice assessments, structured symptom and risk-factor questionnaires, and
automated processing of screening results [29]. Within this workflow, the application provides in-app
instructions for performance tasks, including tremor, balance, gait, voice, and finger-dexterity or touchscreen
