The present modelling analysis focused on five smartphone motor-task datasets relevant to digital motor
assessment: Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, Balance, GaitWalk, and PinchToSize. These tasks were selected
because they capture complementary motor domains relevant to PD-related motor impairment, including
tremor, standing balance, walking-related movement, and touchscreen fine-motor interaction. Participants in
the broader implementation workflow could skip individual tasks or discontinue specific components while
continuing the assessment; therefore, task-level missingness was expected in this real-world app-derived
dataset [29]. Supplementary Table S1 summarises the acquisition method for each analysed task.
Signal Processing and Windowing
Structured CheckPD application data were stored as JSON records within the platform infrastructure, with
curated diagnostic labels managed in the analytic data warehouse as described in the national implementation
programme [29]. The present analysis used de-identified task-level exports from this infrastructure.
Mobile Data-Collection Libraries and IMU Data Schema
Smartphone inertial measurement unit (IMU) data were collected within the CheckPD mobile application
using the Flutter sensors_plus package, version 7.0.0, on Android and iOS devices [30]. This package
provides a common app-level Dart interface for accelerometer and gyroscope streams across platforms.
Although the underlying native sensor hardware and operating-system implementations may differ across
devices, all IMU recordings were stored using a common six-channel schema consisting of tri-axial
accelerometer and tri-axial gyroscope signals, consistent with prior smartphone inertial-sensing work [31].
Smartphone accelerometer studies also support phone-based inertial sensing as a practical source of
movement time-series data [32], and public inertial-walking datasets in PD further demonstrate the relevance
of structured gait sensor recordings for reproducible model development [33]. Accelerometer values were
stored in m/s2, consistent with the sensors_plus accelerometer event definition [34], and gyroscope values
were stored as angular velocity in rad/s, consistent with the sensors_plus gyroscope event definition [35].
Because smartphone sensor streams may show timing jitter and variable sampling intervals, timestamps were
retained for each sample. The effective sampling rate of each recording was estimated from the median inter
sample interval, and signals were aligned to a fixed 40-Hz analysis grid using linear interpolation when
required. This timestamp-based approach was used because the requested sampling interval may not be
