IMU Preprocessing
The IMU preprocessing pipeline included low-pass filtering, accelerometer gravity-component removal,
orientation-consistency checking, and channel-wise normalisation. First, a fourth-order Butterworth low-pass
filter with a maximum cut-off frequency of 20 Hz was applied to reduce high-frequency sensor noise while
preserving movement-related signal components. The cut-off frequency was constrained to remain below the
Nyquist frequency when required. Second, slow gravity-related components were removed only from
accelerometer channels using a fourth-order Butterworth high-pass filter with a cut-off frequency of 0.5 Hz.
This step was applied because raw smartphone accelerometer signals include both body movement and
gravitational acceleration, a common consideration in smartphone human-activity recognition [31]. Gyroscope
channels measure angular velocity and were therefore not subjected to gravity removal.
Third, accepted IMU recordings were acquired according to task-specific CheckPD instructions that specified
a standardised phone orientation and device-axis convention. Before analysis, recordings were checked for
orientation consistency according to the expected task-specific device-frame axis pattern. Recordings with
inconsistent or non-standard orientation were corrected when a deterministic axis transformation was possible;
otherwise, they were excluded during quality control. Therefore, IMU channels were analysed in a
standardised device-centric coordinate frame. Because axes were not transformed into anatomical or global
coordinates, axis-level findings should be interpreted as task-specific device-frame signal patterns rather than
anatomical-axis biomarkers. Fourth, each channel was standardised using z-score normalisation to reduce
inter-device and inter-channel scale differences and improve model-training stability, consistent with common
inertial-sensor activity-recognition preprocessing practice [38]. Smartphone activity-recognition surveys also
emphasise variability across devices and sensing contexts [39].
