Device Effects and Quality Control
Although using Flutter-based app-level sensor and touch-event interfaces reduced platform-level API
inconsistency between Android and iOS, residual device-model effects, sensor calibration differences,
sampling irregularity, and phone-orientation effects were not fully eliminated. These effects were mitigated
through timestamp-based alignment, common unit handling, orientation-consistency checking, channel-wise
normalisation, and participant-level evaluation, but they remain limitations requiring external validation
across device models. No dedicated stratified analysis by operating system, device manufacturer, or sensor
model was performed in this analysis. All preprocessing parameters were estimated within training folds only
to prevent information leakage.
Quality control procedures excluded trials with sensor dropout or duration below 90% of the nominal protocol
length for fixed-duration 20-second IMU tasks, corresponding to less than 18 seconds of usable signal for
Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, or Balance. The nominal duration reported in the acquisition table and the
quality-control threshold refer to the same raw task protocol; no task-specific exception was applied to
Balance. For the PinchToSize task, each recording comprised five sessions targeting circle diameters of 200,
250, 300, 200, and 270 pixels.
