Model Development and Ensembles
Architecture Scope and Rationale
BiLSTM and Conv1D-BiLSTM were selected because the CheckPD signals may contain information at both
local and longer temporal scales. BiLSTM was used as a recurrent-only baseline to model temporal
dependencies across IMU windows and touchscreen-event sequences, whereas Conv1D-BiLSTM was used to
test whether a convolutional front end could additionally extract short local patterns, such as tremor
oscillations, postural-sway fluctuations, gait-related rhythms, or touchscreen adjustment dynamics, before
recurrent aggregation. This controlled comparison therefore assessed the incremental value of convolutional
feature extraction while retaining a comparable recurrent component. The novelty of the present study does
not lie in proposing a new neural-network architecture, but in applying and comparing these models across
five complementary CheckPD motor tasks using participant-level splitting before windowing, fold-contained
preprocessing, out-of-fold stacked ensemble fusion, and evaluation on a common internal held-out cohort.
This combination distinguishes the study from prior work that evaluated related architectures or individual
digital motor modalities without the same CheckPD multidomain and leakage-aware modelling framework.
BiLSTM Architecture
BiLSTM was used as the recurrent sequence-classification model for task-specific smartphone motor time
series. For IMU tasks, the input consisted of 2.5-second windows sampled on the 40-Hz analysis grid with six
channels: ax, ay, az, gx, gy, and gz. For PinchToSize, the model used the seven standardised touchscreen time
series features described above.
Input sequences were processed by stacked bidirectional LSTM layers. The number of recurrent layers,
hidden size, dropout rate, and optional attention module were selected through random-search hyperparameter
optimization. The resulting temporal representation was passed to a multilayer classifier to generate class
probabilities for PD and healthy-control labels. A schematic of the BiLSTM architecture is provided in
Supplementary Fig. S1.
Conv1D-BiLSTM Architecture
In this study, Conv1D-BiLSTM denotes a one-dimensional convolutional temporal feature-extraction front
end followed by bidirectional LSTM temporal modelling. It is not a canonical ConvLSTM recurrent cell, in
which convolutional operations are incorporated into recurrent gates for spatiotemporal sequence modelling
[45]. The term Conv1D-BiLSTM is used here to describe the implemented pipeline architecture: local
temporal motif extraction by Conv1D layers followed by recurrent temporal aggregation using stacked
BiLSTM layers.
