Ensemble Modelling
Inputs to the Ensemble
To evaluate whether different smartphone motor-task domains provided complementary information, we
constructed stacked ensemble models using out-of-fold (OOF) predicted probabilities from the task-specific
base models. This followed the stacked-generalization principle, in which a second-stage learner is trained on
predictions generated by first-stage models [46, 47]. The use of OOF predictions ensured that the meta-learner
was trained using predictions from participants who were not seen by the corresponding base model during
that fold.
Each task-specific base classifier produced two softmax probabilities: one for the healthy-control class and
one for the PD class. Let s index participants, r index recordings or files within participant s, j index windows
or touchscreen sessions within recording r, m index modality, and c ∈ {healthy control, PD} index class. For
each task-specific base model, the softmax probability for window or session j was denoted as psrjm(c).
Recording-level probabilities were first obtained by averaging the available window-level or session-level
probabilities within each file for each class. Here, nsrm denotes the number of windows or sessions available
for recording r from participant s in modality m. For participants with more than one accepted recording
within the same modality, recording-level probabilities were then averaged across accepted recordings to
obtain a participant-level modality probability. Here, Nsm denotes the number of accepted recordings for
participant s in modality m.
