Ensemble Configurations
The ensemble analysis was designed to compare task sets with different levels of participant burden and
motor-domain coverage. The practical difficulty of each task was classified based on an internal survey of
CheckPD data-collection staff and members of the clinical and research team at the Chulalongkorn Centre of
Excellence for Parkinson’s Disease and Related Disorders. Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, and Balance were
classified as easy, GaitWalk as moderate, and PinchToSize as difficult, based on the practical effort required to
explain, administer, and complete each task. These categories reflected task burden and feasibility rather than
model performance. Four configurations were defined:
 Easy: Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, and Balance.
 Easy + Moderate: Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, Balance, and GaitWalk.
 All-task: Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, Balance, GaitWalk, and PinchToSize.
 Main Symptoms: Rest Tremor, GaitWalk, and PinchToSize, representing tremor, gait, and
fine-motor/touchscreen domains.
For each configuration, participant-level OOF probabilities from the included task-specific classifiers were
concatenated and used as inputs to the stacked meta-learner. This design allowed comparison of low-burden,
expanded, all-task, and symptom-focused fusion strategies.
Modality Intersection and Feature Construction
For each ensemble configuration, only participants with complete OOF probability data for every included
modality were retained for meta-model training. In the Main Symptoms configuration, for example, the
complete-case intersection required Rest Tremor, GaitWalk, and PinchToSize probabilities from the same
participant. These modality-intersection counts refer to the development-set OOF tables used for ensemble
meta-model training and should not be interpreted as held-out test denominators. They are summarised in
Supplementary Table S2.
For each retained complete-case participant, the ensemble feature vector was constructed by concatenating the
healthy-control and PD probabilities for each included modality. Because the base models used two-class
softmax outputs, the ensemble input dimension was d_in = 2 × M, where M denotes the number of modalities
included in the ensemble configuration. For example, an ensemble containing three modalities had six input
features.
