Meta-Model Architecture and Training
A small feed-forward neural network was trained as the ensemble meta-learner. The network consisted of two
hidden layers with batch normalisation, ReLU activation, and dropout, followed by a sigmoid output layer for
binary classification. The implemented hidden-layer widths were 128 and 64, with dropout probability 0.1.
The meta-learner produced a single output probability for the PD class.
The meta-model was optimised using binary cross-entropy loss with the Adam optimizer, a learning rate of
10−4, and weight decay of 10−2. Training was performed for up to 200 epochs with early stopping using a
patience of 20 epochs. Within each OOF-derived ensemble training table, a stratified 90/10 split was used for
validation and early stopping.
Training, Validation, and Statistical Analysis
The experimental pipeline began by grouping all raw task recordings by participant identifier. All train,
validation, and test partitions were created at the participant level before preprocessing-parameter estimation,
normalisation, windowing or session construction, augmentation, and model fitting. This participant-level
partitioning was used to prevent within-participant leakage, ensuring that windows, touchscreen sessions, or
repeated recordings from the same participant did not appear in more than one data partition.
Within each training fold, preprocessing parameters, normalisation statistics, windowing or session
construction, data augmentation, and model fitting were performed using training participants only. An
internal validation split within the training fold was used for early stopping and random-search
hyperparameter selection. Hyperparameters were selected through 25 iterations of random search over
predefined ranges, using validation ROC AUC as the optimisation objective. The full hyperparameter search
