Validation and Performance Analysis
Model development used stratified 5-fold cross-validation at the participant level to preserve class balance and
prevent within-participant leakage. In each fold, approximately 80% of participants were used for model
development, including an internal validation split for early stopping and hyperparameter selection, and
approximately 20% were held out for out-of-fold prediction generation. All preprocessing parameters,
normalisation statistics, augmentation procedures, and model weights were estimated or applied using the
development-training partition only. Validation data, out-of-fold prediction generation, SHAP explanation
samples, and held-out internal test data were not augmented.
Out-of-fold predictions from the fold-specific development-set evaluation partitions were used to construct
ensemble-training inputs following the stacked-generalization principle [46, 47]. Participant-level splitting
was applied throughout because repeated windows, touchscreen sessions, or recordings from the same
participant are correlated and can introduce subject-level data leakage if they appear in both training and
evaluation partitions. Such leakage can inflate biomedical prediction performance and lead to overoptimistic
conclusions [23].
