Results
Participant and Task Availability
The analysis included 679 participants, comprising 344 neurologist-confirmed PD participants and 335
neurologist-confirmed healthy controls. Task-level data availability is summarised in Supplementary Fig. S3.
Because this was a retrospective case-control modelling analysis using a curated labelled dataset, model
performance should be interpreted as internal case-control discrimination from smartphone motor time series,
rather than as population-screening effectiveness, calibrated risk prediction, or evidence of clinical
deployment readiness.
The exploratory demographic sensitivity analysis using age and gender alone showed limited discrimination,
with ROC AUC 0.5784, PR-AUC 0.6170, Brier score 0.2422, accuracy 59.68%, sensitivity 41.29%, and
specificity 75.66%. These results suggest that age and gender alone did not account for the stronger
discrimination observed in the smartphone motor time-series models, although residual demographic
confounding from measured or unmeasured factors cannot be fully excluded.
