BiLSTM Versus Conv1D-BiLSTM Architecture Comparison
In paired task-fold comparisons across the five individual motor tasks, ROC AUC favoured Conv1D-BiLSTM
over BiLSTM in 22 of 25 evaluations (two-sided exact sign test, P<.001). Accuracy and Macro F1 score also
favoured Conv1D-BiLSTM in 22 of 25 evaluations, with P<.001 for both metrics. These findings indicate a
consistent internal performance advantage of the convolutional-recurrent architecture over the recurrent-only
architecture for task-specific classification.
This advantage was most evident for the four IMU-based tasks. Conv1D-BiLSTM models achieved higher
ROC AUC, Macro F1 score, and accuracy than the corresponding BiLSTM models for Rest Tremor, Postural
Tremor, Balance, and GaitWalk. The largest ROC AUC improvements were observed for Balance, increasing
from 0.6675 to 0.8627; Postural Tremor, from 0.6813 to 0.8750; and GaitWalk, from 0.7090 to 0.8700. These
results suggest that the Conv1D front end helped extract local temporal motifs from short multichannel IMU
windows before recurrent temporal aggregation. In contrast, PinchToSize showed mixed architecture
dependent results, suggesting that event-driven touchscreen sequences may differ from fixed-duration IMU
signals in their optimal modelling architecture.
