SHAP Model-Reliance Findings
SHAP analyses were interpreted as post hoc model-reliance patterns rather than causal explanations or
independently validated feature-importance findings. Across task-specific Conv1D-BiLSTM models,
attribution patterns were broadly consistent with the structure of the corresponding motor tasks (Table 4). The
Rest Tremor model showed a gyroscope-dominant reliance pattern, particularly involving gyro_x and gyro_y,
with smaller accelerometer contributions. The Postural Tremor model also relied prominently on gyroscope
channels, with additional accelerometer contribution, especially acc_x and acc_z. The Balance model showed
multiaxial IMU reliance, with stronger accelerometer contribution and secondary gyroscope involvement. The
GaitWalk model relied on both accelerometer and gyroscope channels, suggesting use of linear-acceleration
and angular-velocity information during walking. For PinchToSize, the most influential inputs were
touchscreen targeting, scaling, and timing features, including dist_to_target, diameter, dist_to_target_pct,
timestamp, and expansion_rate.
Overall, the task-specific SHAP patterns support the face validity of the learned representations by showing
that each model relied mainly on inputs relevant to its corresponding motor task. These distinct reliance
patterns may also help explain why combining different motor domains provided complementary information
in the ensemble models. SHAP values were computed on model-development samples, the input channels
were temporally correlated, and IMU axes remained in a device-centric coordinate frame. Therefore, these
attribution patterns indicate internal model reliance rather than causal physiology, anatomical-axis biomarkers,
or independently validated digital motor biomarkers. Full post hoc SHAP beeswarm plots are provided in
Supplementary Fig. S8.
