Discussion
Principal Findings
This study evaluated recurrent, convolutional-recurrent, and stacked-ensemble modelling of CheckPD App
smartphone IMU and touchscreen motor-task time series. Conv1D-BiLSTM models outperformed BiLSTM
baselines across the four IMU-based tasks, whereas PinchToSize showed mixed architecture-dependent
results. Multimodal stacking suggested complementarity across tremor, balance, gait, and touchscreen fine
motor domains: the Main Symptoms ensemble achieved the highest-ranking performance, while the lower
burden Easy ensemble achieved the strongest binary classification performance. Together, these findings
indicate that brief smartphone motor tasks contain PD-related information suitable for further technical
evaluation, while remaining limited to internal case–control discrimination in the present study.
The public-dataset benchmarks provided additional task-domain context. ROC AUC values ranged from
0.7333 to 0.9000 across retrained PADS and WearGait-PD analyses, with tremor-domain results broadly
aligned with CheckPD internal reference values and strong GaitWalk discrimination in WearGait-PD. The
Balance domain showed the largest between-dataset difference among the benchmarked tasks, with ROC
AUC 0.8627 (95% CI 0.7717–0.9415) in CheckPD and 0.7333 (95% CI 0.3996–1.0000) in WearGait-PD. The
95% CIs overlapped. Differences in acquisition protocol, sensor setup, device characteristics, and cohort
composition may have contributed to this variation; however, the present benchmarking design cannot isolate
the contribution of any individual factor. Accordingly, this difference should be interpreted descriptively rather
than as evidence of a statistically established difference in model performance. Because models were retrained
separately within each public dataset, these analyses should be interpreted as task-domain benchmarking
rather than frozen-model external validation.
Interpretation and Comparison With Prior Work
The findings are directionally consistent with prior smartphone and wearable-sensor studies showing that
motion, gait, finger-motor, and touchscreen-derived signals can support PD classification or early motor
impairment detection. Reviews of automated and AI-enabled PD detection highlight the diversity of
modalities, datasets, algorithms, and generalisation challenges in this field [11-13]. Related studies have
evaluated smartphone walking recordings, hand-drawing traces, LSTM and GRU architectures, quantitative
finger-dexterity time-series analysis, smartphone touch gestures, mobile-based PD assessments, gait time
