The present study extends this literature by evaluating CheckPD App motor tasks collected within a national
digital screening context, combining IMU-derived tremor, balance, and gait tasks with a touchscreen fine
motor interaction task. However, direct numerical comparison with previous studies is inappropriate because
studies differ substantially in recruitment setting, disease stage, control-group definition, sensor placement,
task protocol, validation design, and handling of repeated windows or sessions from the same participant.
Supplementary Table S11 provides a descriptive comparison with selected prior digital PD studies.
The architecture comparison suggests that the Conv1D front end was useful for short multichannel IMU
windows, where local temporal motifs may reflect tremor oscillations, postural sway, or rhythmic gait-related
movement before recurrent temporal aggregation. The mixed PinchToSize results are also clinically and
technically plausible, because PinchToSize is an event-driven touchscreen interaction task rather than a fixed
rate inertial recording. Its features represent touchscreen scaling accuracy, inter-event timing, and adjustment
dynamics, which may not benefit from convolutional temporal feature extraction in the same way as IMU
signals.
The ensemble results reinforce the multidomain nature of PD motor expression. No single smartphone task is
expected to capture all clinically relevant manifestations. The Main Symptoms ensemble, combining Rest
Tremor, GaitWalk, and PinchToSize, achieved the highest ROC AUC and PR-AUC, whereas the Easy
ensemble, combining Rest Tremor, Postural Tremor, and Balance, achieved the strongest Macro F1 score and
accuracy. This distinction is important for future evaluation because the optimal task set may depend on
whether the intended priority is ranking discrimination, binary classification, participant burden, task
feasibility, or robustness to missingness.
The PD cohort had a mean MDS-UPDRS Part III score of 11.84 (SD 12.61), but these summary values alone
do not establish the severity distribution. The overall ROC AUC values do not show whether discrimination
was driven mainly by participants with greater motor impairment or would be similar in a mild-PD group.
Future validation should assess performance by motor severity, with consistent recording of medication state
and disease stage.
