touchscreen-based fine-motor interaction domain. The main strengths of the study include neurologist
confirmed PD and healthy-control labels, multiple smartphone motor domains, participant-level partitioning
before window or session construction, fold-contained preprocessing and model development, OOF ensemble
training, complete-case multimodal fusion, and held-out internal evaluation on a balanced 90-participant
cohort.
Limitations
These strengths should be interpreted alongside important limitations. This was a retrospective case–control
model-development study, not a diagnostic validation study. The control group consisted of neurologist
confirmed healthy controls rather than symptomatic differential-diagnosis controls; therefore, performance
against essential tremor, atypical parkinsonism, drug-induced parkinsonism, vascular parkinsonism,
neuropathy, frailty, arthritis, vestibular disorders, or musculoskeletal gait disorders remains unknown. The
held-out cohort was internal, balanced, and complete-case, which may not reflect real-world screening
prevalence or task-completion patterns. The held-out cohort size was determined pragmatically from
complete-case availability rather than through a formal sample-size calculation. Although the balanced 90
participant design enabled consistent comparison of all task and ensemble configurations, the resulting
performance estimates remain subject to sampling uncertainty; for participant-level classification, one
observation corresponds to approximately 1.1 percentage points of accuracy. Larger independent validation
cohorts will therefore be required for more precise performance estimation. Development-set task missingness
and complete-case ensemble training may have favoured participants who were better able to complete the
required tasks. Additional limitations include unmeasured device effects, incomplete operating-system or
device-model auditing, lack of probability calibration, and retrained public-dataset benchmarking rather than
frozen external validation. Exact dopaminergic medication ON/OFF state at task recording was not
