Future Work
Future work should prioritise prospective multisite external validation across diverse smartphones, operating
systems, clinical settings, and representative screening populations. Such studies should include symptomatic
non-PD controls, structured medication-state capture, device and protocol auditing, prespecified subgroup
analyses, usability assessment, and linkage to clinician-rated severity outcomes [48, 52]. Prospective
validation should also capture assistance required, device type, operating system, task environment, task
failure reasons, and participant burden, so that missingness and performance can be interpreted alongside real
world feasibility. Calibration under realistic screening prevalence will be essential before any operating
threshold can be proposed for patient-level use. Clinical utility should also be evaluated using net-benefit
approaches, such as decision-curve analysis, to determine whether model-informed assessment would
improve referral decisions compared with usual practice across plausible risk thresholds [53]. For
PinchToSize specifically, future studies should examine its relationship with MDS-UPDRS finger tapping,
hand movements, pronation–supination, functional dexterity measures, and PDQ-8 or related quality-of-life
outcomes [17, 44, 54]. These steps are necessary before the approach can be considered for future screening
support evaluation in real-world settings.
Conclusion
In this retrospective internal validation study of CheckPD App data, deep sequence modelling of smartphone IMU and touchscreen motor-task time series showed promising internal case-control discrimination between neurologist-confirmed PD participants and neurologist-confirmed healthy controls. Conv1D-BiLSTM models improved performance across most IMU tasks compared with BiLSTM models, and multimodal neural stacking suggested complementarity across tremor, balance, gait, and touchscreen fine-motor interaction domains. These findings support further evaluation of recurrent and convolutional-recurrent models as a technical approach for extracting PD-related information from smartphone motor time series. They do not establish diagnostic validity, calibrated clinical risk prediction, differential-diagnosis performance, or readiness for patient-level use.
